.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLOG 4
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm klog
.Nd kernel message buffer device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/klog
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel keeps every message it prints in a circular buffer of
.Va kern.msgbufsize
bytes, 64 KiB by default; when the buffer is full, the oldest bytes are
overwritten.
Messages printed before the kernel's memory allocator is ready are kept too,
and moved into the buffer once it is.
User space cannot write to the buffer.
.Pp
The character device
.Pa /dev/klog ,
number 7, 0, owned by root with mode 0600, reads the buffer for
.Xr syslogd 8 .
Each line is stored as the kernel printed it, with a
.Ql \&< Ns Ar priority Ns \&>
prefix when the code printing it gave one.
.Pp
Only one descriptor may have
.Pa /dev/klog
open at a time.
A
.Xr read 2
returns the bytes nobody has read yet, as many as fit, and consumes them; if
some unread bytes have been overwritten, reading resumes at the oldest byte
still held, and the lost ones are skipped.
With nothing unread it waits for the kernel to print, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
if the descriptor is non-blocking.
A signal interrupts the wait.
.Xr poll 2
reports the descriptor readable when there is unread data.
.Pp
Reading
.Pa /dev/klog
does not change what
.Xr dmesg 8
shows: it reads the whole buffer through the
.Va kern.msgbuf
sysctl, and
.Va kern.msgbuf_clear
empties that view without affecting
.Pa /dev/klog .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/klog -compact
.It Pa /dev/klog
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Xr open 2 :
another descriptor has the device open.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
.Xr read 2 :
nothing is unread and the descriptor is non-blocking.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Xr write 2
was called.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 3 ,
.Xr dmesg 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Pa /dev/klog
device first appeared in
.Bx 4.3 .
OxideBSD's follows the BSDs': exclusive and consuming, with the whole buffer
also readable through
.Va kern.msgbuf .
